//============================================================================
// ant_coverage_control.svrf --- PROJECT-OWNED coverage control for the
// foundry antenna run.  Contains no foundry rule text.
//
// A joint work commissioned on behalf of SoC Labs, under Arm Academic Access
// license.  Copyright 2026, SoC Labs (www.soclabs.org)
//============================================================================
// APPENDED BY run_ant.sh AFTER the foundry deck's INCLUDE, never before: every
// layer named below is DERIVED BY THAT DECK and does not exist until it has
// been read.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
// --------------------
// This repository has a documented class of check that "passed" while
// measuring nothing -- a rule evaluated over a layer that is empty in the
// stream returns zero, and zero is indistinguishable from clean.  It has
// happened here at least five separate times.  An antenna run is unusually
// exposed to it, because the denominator of every antenna rule is TRANSISTOR
// GATE AREA, and this project streams LEF abstracts for every standard cell,
// IO cell and bond pad.  Those cells contribute no gate area at all.  A run
// that found nothing but had nothing to find would look exactly like a pass.
//
// So the run carries its own population census, in the same summary report,
// produced by the same Calibre invocation over the same layout.  A reader
// cannot get to the antenna zeros without walking past these.
//
// HOW TO READ THE COUNTS
// ----------------------
// The wrapper sets a finite DRC MAXIMUM RESULTS, so a count that equals that
// limit is a FLOOR and not a total -- Calibre writes the truncated value into
// both count fields, so a saturated check is not visibly different from an
// exact one.  That is fine for the question being asked here, which is only
// "is this population non-empty", and it is NOT fine for any other question.
// Do not quote these as populations.  For exact per-layer populations of the
// stream itself use scripts/ci/gds_layer_census.py, which reads the GDSII
// records directly and cannot saturate.
//
// WHY THESE ARE ALL `COPY` AND NOT A RATIO
// ---------------------------------------
// The obvious control -- a real NET AREA RATIO with the limit pushed down so
// that any net carrying both terms must trip it -- CANNOT BE WRITTEN HERE, and
// the reason is worth recording because it will be re-proposed.
//
// The foundry deck sets DRC INCREMENTAL CONNECT YES and declares the metal
// stack as a sequence of LAYOUT TOP LAYER steps.  Connectivity is therefore
// POSITIONAL: each metal's antenna rulechecks sit between the CONNECT that
// brings that metal into the net and the CONNECT that brings the next one in.
// By the end of the file the low metals are no longer in the connectivity set
// at all, and a ratio referencing one gets
//     ERROR: Error EXT3 ... cannot establish connectivity of this layer in the
//     MASK connectivity set: M1
// which is what the first attempt at this file produced (measured 2026-08-26,
// Calibre exits 4 in about twenty seconds -- it never reads the layout).
// An append can only carry checks that need no connectivity.  That is what
// COPY is.
//
// The discrimination question those ratios were meant to answer -- "could this
// deck have reported a violation at all?" -- is answered instead by a fixture
// that runs the SAME foundry deck over a tiny synthetic layout built to carry
// one, in BOTH directions: ci/fixtures/antenna-deck-fires/.  Keeping the two
// apart is better anyway: a discrimination control that rides on the signoff
// run costs a 300 MB stream read to answer a question about the deck.
//
// WHAT EACH ONE DEMONSTRATES
// --------------------------
//   ANTCTL.GATE.POPULATION  the deck's own antenna DENOMINATOR, GATE.  Empty
//                           => every antenna rulecheck in this run divided by
//                           nothing and its zero is vacuous.  This is THE one
//                           to read first.
//   ANTCTL.SD.POPULATION    the diffusion (diode) term.  Empty => the
//                           diode-protection variants measured nothing either.
//   ANTCTL.POLY.POPULATION  the other half of GATE, and the numerator of the
//                           two POLY antenna rules.
//   ANTCTL.OD.POPULATION    the other half of GATE.  GATE = OD AND POLY, so a
//                           populated OD with an empty GATE would say the two
//                           are present but never overlap -- a different and
//                           much stranger failure than either being absent.
//   ANTCTL.M1.POPULATION    the lowest routing numerator.
//   ANTCTL.M9.POPULATION    the highest routing numerator.
//   ANTCTL.AP.POPULATION    the RDL numerator, which is the one layer in this
//                           stream that no standard cell contributes to, so it
//                           is populated or the stream is wrong.
//============================================================================

ANTCTL.GATE.POPULATION {@ CONTROL (project-owned): is the antenna denominator non-empty?
   COPY GATE
}

ANTCTL.SD.POPULATION {@ CONTROL (project-owned): is the diffusion/diode term non-empty?
   COPY SD
}

ANTCTL.POLY.POPULATION {@ CONTROL (project-owned): gate poly present?
   COPY POLY
}

ANTCTL.OD.POPULATION {@ CONTROL (project-owned): active area present?
   COPY OD
}

ANTCTL.M1.POPULATION {@ CONTROL (project-owned): lowest routing layer present?
   COPY M1
}

ANTCTL.M9.POPULATION {@ CONTROL (project-owned): highest routing layer present?
   COPY M9
}

ANTCTL.AP.POPULATION {@ CONTROL (project-owned): redistribution layer present?
   COPY AP
}
